  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of gene regulation in 3D chromatin. DNA is drawn as a long fiber folded into large loops between dense chromatin regions, with smaller local loops bringing distant regulatory elements into contact. Along the DNA, dark boxes mark exons, gray arrows indicate promoter activity, and red arrows show enhancer action. Colored shapes represent transcription factors or co-activators distributed within broad, lightly shaded regulatory neighborhoods.\n\nThe main idea is that gene expression depends on spatial proximity rather than simple linear distance on the chromosome. Enhancers located far away along the DNA can loop over and act on promoters when the chromatin folds appropriately, helping drive transcription and production of mRNA. Several genes appear to sit within shared contact domains, where multiple enhancers and protein complexes interact with different promoters. Some loops seem to connect one enhancer-rich region to more than one gene, suggesting coordinated regulation, while other promoter arrows indicate more local promoter-driven effects.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that genome folding organizes regulatory contacts, allowing transcription factors and co-activators to concentrate within local hubs and influence which genes are transcribed.",
